*(
    title: Proof by Contradiction
    description: Assuming a statement is false, deriving an absurdity from it, and concluding that it was true.
    tags: proof, contradiction, irrationality, prime numbers, extension 2, stub
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Some statements resist a direct attack because they assert an absence: that no fraction squares to two, that no largest prime exists. There is nothing to construct and nothing to compute, so a direct argument has no purchase.

    Contradiction supplies the purchase. Assume the statement false, and the assumption becomes a concrete object to work with — a fraction in lowest terms, a complete list of primes. Written out here: the structure of the method, the irrationality of the square root of two, Euclid's argument on the primes, and the care needed in negating a statement before assuming it.
}
